# Findings that must not fail the build, each with a reason and a date.
#
# This file is applied only to the steps that fail the pipeline. The scans that publish
# to the Security tab do not use it, so an entry here hides nothing: it says that the
# finding is known and cannot be acted on today.
#
# An entry without exp: never expires and does not belong here. The date is what forces
# somebody to look again.

# gRPC-Go: xDS RBAC and HTTP/2 vulnerabilities, in google.golang.org/grpc v1.80.0 as
# statically linked into the official rclone binary. Fixed in grpc 1.82.1, but rclone
# 1.74.4 of 2026-07-08 is the latest release and still ships the old one: there is no
# version of rclone to move to.
#
# It does not apply to how rclone is used here. The advisory affects a gRPC server that
# accepts connections and enforces xDS RBAC policies, which is rclone's rc daemon
# (rclone rcd, rclone serve). This project only ever starts one-shot subprocesses,
# version, listremotes, lsjson and cat, from backend/app/rclone/client.py. No socket is
# opened and no gRPC code runs at all.
#
# Recheck when a new rclone is released: if it links grpc 1.82.1 or later, bump
# RCLONE_VERSION in backend/Dockerfile and delete these lines.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-10-31

# golang.org/x/text: norm.Iter can loop for ever on input holding invalid UTF-8 bytes,
# in v0.38.0 as statically linked into the official rclone binary. Fixed in x/text
# 0.39.0, and rclone master already carries 0.40.0, but 1.74.4 of 2026-07-08 is still
# the latest release: as with grpc above, there is no version of rclone to move to.
#
# Unlike the grpc entry this one is not unreachable, and it is not claimed to be. rclone
# normalises the names it reads, so a file whose name holds invalid UTF-8 on a remote
# could in principle reach the loop. What it can cost is bounded: rclone runs here only
# as one-shot subprocesses, each under the timeouts in config.py, so the worst outcome
# is a scan that hangs until its ceiling and a job that fails. No memory is corrupted,
# nothing is executed and nothing is disclosed. The names come from the user's own
# remotes in a self-hosted application, so there is no third party to feed them either.
#
# Recheck at the same time as the grpc entry: master has both fixes, so the next rclone
# release should clear the two of them together.
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-10-31
GHSA-jpjm-c3r5-q96r exp:2026-10-31
